 "title": "Use consumer imagination in advertising",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "This clip from the show 'Mad Men' illustrates a strategy for creating impactful advertisements by focusing on consumer imagination rather than explicit imagery. The speaker argues that by leaving an advertisement 'tantalisingly incomplete,' consumers are forced to fill in the blanks, which makes the message more memorable.",
 "key_points": [
  "Focus on consumer imagination instead of just showing the product or a picture.",
  "Don't show the product in the initial ad creative; let the audience mentally connect the dots.",
  "The creator claims that effective ads don't need a massive budget or time limit if they engage the consumer's imagination.",
  "Leaving out elements makes an ad 'tantalisingly incomplete,' which encourages active consumer engagement.",
  "Bold, simple creative can be more powerful than showing the brand bottle directly."
 ],
 "content": "Text overlay: Now that's how you do advertising. Clean and simple. #MadMen. Transcript: Speaker 1: It's clean, it's simple and it's tantalisingly incomplete. What's missing? One thing. Speaker 2: One thing. Speaker 1: Pass the HEINZ. Pass the Heinz. Speaker 3: You mean the Heinz ketchup? Speaker 1: It's Heinz. It only means one thing. Speaker 2: It feels like half an ad. Speaker 1: The greatest thing you have working for you is not the photo you take or the picture you paint. It's the imagination of the consumer. They have no budget. They have no time limit. And if you can get into that space, your ad can run all day. Speaker 2: Well, Pete, you said I'd say it. It's pretty bold work. I think I still want to see our bottle. Speaker 1: I thought that at first, too, but if you- Speaker 2: We will test it both ways. Speaker 1: It's a testament to ketchup that there can be no confusion. Speaker 2: Let me chew on it, fellas. Speaker 1: You're going to be thinking of ketchup all day, and you didn't even see it. Text shown on screen: Pass the HEINZ.",
